{{Constitution of Balisca sidebar}}

The '''Basic Law: Political Parties, Organization and Representation''' ({{lang-bl|Ley Fundamental: Partidos Políticos, Organización y Representación}}), is a [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Law]] of [[Balisca]] which established the confederation as a {{W|non-partisan democracy}}, established {{W|restrictions on political parties}} and political {{W|lobbying}} and reformed the electoral process. 

The Amendment dissolved all active political parties within Balisca, regardless of their ideology and size, and prohibited the future establishment of political parties. Since the passage of the amendment via {{W|referendum}}, delegate candidates for election to the [[Confederal Congress]] have stood in a personal capacity. 

In place of political parties, [[List of platforms in Balisca|platforms]] such as the [[Revolutionary Front]], [[Cosmopolitan Collective]], [[Ecological Action]] and [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] have emerged. 

== Content ==

==== Preamble ====
''Believing that the institution of the political party has, over time, become a barrier between the representative and the represented; seeking to dismantle the divisive machinery of partisanship and the corrupting influence of political lobbying; and in order to restore the primacy of the individual conscience in legislative matters and return power to the citizens and their directly accountable delegates, the people of Balisca, by referendum, enact this Basic Law.''

=== Article 1: Establishment of a Non-Partisan Democracy ===
The Baliscan Confederation is hereby established as a non-partisan democracy. All elections for the Confederal Congress, and for cantonal and communal bodies, shall be conducted on a non-partisan basis.

=== Article 2: Dissolution of Existing Political Parties ===
a) As of the date of the enactment of this Basic Law, all political parties previously operating within Balisca are hereby dissolved. Their legal status as political entities is terminated. b) All dissolved parties shall have a period of one hundred and eighty (180) days to liquidate their assets, settle their debts, and cease all political operations. Assets may be transferred to non-political, charitable, or educational foundations, subject to oversight by the Confederal Electoral Commission.

=== Article 3: Prohibition on the Formation of Political Parties ===

# The formation of any future political party is prohibited. 
# For the purposes of this law, a "political party" is defined as any organization that exhibits a majority of the following characteristics: <br>  '''I'''. Possesses a permanent, hierarchical leadership structure that exercises binding authority over its members. <br> '''II'''. Compels its members who are elected to public office to vote according to a unified, centrally-determined directive (a "party line" or "whip system"). <br> '''III'''. Endorses and fields candidates for election under a common, registered organizational name that appears on the ballot. iv. Maintains a formal, dues-paying membership structure as a requirement for participation in its governance.

=== Article 4: Individual Candidacy and Representation ===

# All candidates for election to the Confederal Congress must stand in a personal capacity. 
# Ballots shall list only the candidate's name, canton of residence, and a brief, personally authored statement of principles. The use of any party, platform, or collective branding on the ballot itself is forbidden.

=== Article 5: Restrictions on Political Lobbying ===
Direct lobbying of elected delegates by corporate entities, trade unions, and other formal organizations is hereby restricted. The Confederal Congress is mandated to pass enabling legislation to define the precise limits of such activities, with the goal of privileging direct communication from individual citizens and communal representatives.

=== Article 6: Enactment ===
This Basic Law shall come into full force and effect upon its successful ratification by a majority of the valid votes cast in a confederation-wide referendum.

